1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.200
In this lesson, you will

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.900
build a number of increasingly complex
workflows to master the basic concepts.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.933
You'll start with a simple workflow
consisting of a series of steps

4
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:14.200
that emit events and then add to it
branching and looping logic, implement

5
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.600
concurrent execution,

6
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.600
and finally collect
events of different types at a given step.

7
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.000
All right,
let's dive into the code. Under the hood,

8
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.400
workflows are regular Python classes.

9
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.800
They are defined as a series of steps,
each of which receives

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.200
certain classes of events and emits
certain classes of events.

11
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:33.733
So let's get started building one.

12
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:37.833
First, we'll bring in our imports
and we're going to be using OpenAI today.

13
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.733
So let's bring in our OpenAI key
to define our events,

14
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.033
we need to import some special events
plus the core of workflows.

15
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.733
That's what we're doing here.

16
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:50.200
Now, let's define the workflow itself.

17
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.600
When you've got here is a regular class
declaration, a step decorator,

18
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.300
and an async function called My Step.

19
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.200
In my step, we've defined an event
that is of a specific type,

20
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.000
a start event, and we've also said that
it emits a specific

21
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.200
type of event called a stop event.

22
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.033
These are both special events.

23
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:14.600
The start event is triggered
when the workflow starts

24
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.700
and when a stop event is triggered,
a workflow will automatically stop.

25
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.633
The async keyword defines
asynchronous functions,

26
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:26.100
which can be paused and resumed, allowing
other tasks to run in the meantime.

27
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.233
We'll see how this is useful
when we're doing stuff in parallel later.

28
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.133
You can instantiate and run your workflow
like this.

29
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.833
We just call my workflow

30
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:41.400
with a timeout of 10s, and in this case
we're going to set verbose to false.

31
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.833
But we're going to set it to true later.

32
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:44.833
And you'll see what the difference is
then.

33
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:48.400
Once you've got your workflow
you call dot run on it.

34
00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:51.733
And we're using the await keyword
because this is asynchronous.

35
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.400
And what you get back from the run
command is an asynchronous handle.

36
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.900
And then we can print the result.

37
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.300
So let's execute
all of these cells and see what we get.

38
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.000
Tada! Workflows are asynchronous by default.

39
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.400
So, you use the await
to get the result of the run command.

40
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.633
This is going to work fine in a notebook
environment in a vanilla Python script

41
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:23.300
you're going to need to import asyncio
and wrap your code in an async function.

42
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:25.400
I'll just show you what that looks like.

43
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.400
We can't execute it here because
this is a notebook, so it won't work.

44
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.700
That's what that looks like.

45
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.600
You have exactly the same code

46
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.400
to instantiate and run the function,
but you've wrapped it in an async function

47
00:02:38.933 --> 00:02:41.433
and you've imported asyncio,

48
00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:44.533
and you've used asyncio
to run your async function for you.

49
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:48.100
That's the only difference about
running it in a notebook versus running it

50
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.700
in vanilla Python. Because this code isn't
going to run in the notebook,

51
00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:55.400
I'm going to delete it just so that
we can avoid it accidentally running.

52
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.600
One of the great features of workflows

53
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.033
is the built-in visualizer,
which we're going to import now.

54
00:03:01.333 --> 00:03:04.333
This is the function draw
all possible flows.

55
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.900
It takes two arguments, one of which is
the workflow that you want to visualize,

56
00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:12.400
and the other of which is the file name
where you want it

57
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.133
to output the visualization.

58
00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:17.600
What it outputs is an HTML file

59
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.200
containing an interactive
visualization of your workflow.

60
00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:26.900
Because you would have to download
an HTML file and open it in a browser,

61
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.700
we've set up a helper function
which displays that HTML file

62
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.400
directly in the notebook.

63
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.700
That's what is happening here.

64
00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:37.233
We get the HTML file
and we display it as an HTML element.

65
00:03:38.233 --> 00:03:41.233
Let's see what our workflow looks like.

66
00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:43.000
We get this.

67
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.800
As you can see, it's interactive.

68
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.200
So you can drag these things around.

69
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.033
It's the way that your workflow
is visualized isn't the way you want it.

70
00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:52.700
But this is an extremely basic workflow
with just the one step,

71
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.700
my step, and a start event,
and a stop event.

72
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.433
Of course, a flow with a single step
is not very useful.

73
00:03:58.433 --> 00:04:00.633
Let's define a multi-step workflow.

74
00:04:00.633 --> 00:04:03.800
Multiple steps are created
by defining custom events

75
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.800
that can be emitted by steps and trigger
other steps.

76
00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:11.533
So let's define a simple three-step
workflow by designing two custom events.

77
00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:13.800
First event and second event.

78
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.000
These classes can have any names
and properties,

79
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.000
but they must inherit from event.

80
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.533
That's what you've done there.

81
00:04:22.533 --> 00:04:26.133
Now that we have our events,
we can define a workflow that uses them.

82
00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:31.500
You define a workflow
by defining what events each step accepts

83
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:32.800
and what events

84
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.000
that each step emits. That defines
how data flows around the workflow.

85
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.200
Let's see what that looks like.

86
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.733
So here we have a new

87
00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:44.933
class, My Workflow, with a step defined
as step one.

88
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.500
It accepts a start event
so that one special

89
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.300
and this will kick off the workflow.

90
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.833
And then emits a first event.

91
00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:52.900
Step two: accepts a first event.

92
00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:56.233
So it will be triggered
by the output of step one.

93
00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.833
And it emits a second event which triggers
step three, which emits a stop event.

94
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.100
The stop event will halt the workflow.

95
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.100
You can run this
just like you ran the other workflows.

96
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.500
So let's execute all three of those cells.

97
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.800
As you can see, it started the workflow.

98
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.433
You got the first step, the second step,
and the workflow is complete.

99
00:05:19.633 --> 00:05:22.600
We can visualize this
just like we visualize the last one.

100
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.300
So let's call our visualizer.

101
00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.700
And then let's display what it outputs.

102
00:05:37.233 --> 00:05:39.000
This is a more interesting visualization.

103
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.800
You can see our start event
going to step one

104
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.933
emitting this first event
which triggers step two, which emitted

105
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.400
the second event which triggers step three
which triggers a stop event.

106
00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.433
And then the workflow halts.

107
00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:52.400
However,
there's not much point to a workflow

108
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.300
if you just run it straight through.

109
00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.700
A key feature of workflows
is that they enable branching and looping

110
00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:01.700
logic more simply and flexibly than graph-
based approaches to enable looping.

111
00:06:01.933 --> 00:06:04.300
Let's create a new loop event.

112
00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.600
This isn't a special event.

113
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:06.433
We've just created

114
00:06:06.433 --> 00:06:09.433
an event called the loop event
that we're going to use to do loops.

115
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.533
Now you'll edit your step
one to make a random decision

116
00:06:13.533 --> 00:06:16.533
about whether to execute
serially or to loop back.

117
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.033
So we have a random number generator
that's randomly generating

118
00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:21.433
either a 0 or 1.

119
00:06:21.433 --> 00:06:25.033
If it's a zero, we just say the bad thing
has happened and we loop back.

120
00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.300
If it's a one, we say a good thing
happened and we move on to the next step.

121
00:06:29.533 --> 00:06:32.000
We do the loop by omitting the loop event.

122
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.000
The other change that we've made here is
we've added an accepting type to step one.

123
00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.900
So now it will accept either
a start event or a loop event.

124
00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:44.033
That means that either of these events
will trigger step one.

125
00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.100
We've also changed what types it emits,

126
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.233
so it emits of either a first event
or that loop event.

127
00:06:50.433 --> 00:06:52.200
Now let's execute our loop.

128
00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.400
And we might have to run it
a couple of times because it's random.

129
00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.900
So it might just work the first time.

130
00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.633
Yep. That's exactly what happened.

131
00:06:59.633 --> 00:07:00.733
Let's execute it again.

132
00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:02.833
All right.

133
00:07:02.833 --> 00:07:04.300
Now we see a bad thing happened.

134
00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.800
Then a good thing happened.
Then the first step was complete.

135
00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.500
Then the second step was complete.

136
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.700
Then the workflow was complete.

137
00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.700
Let's visualize that once more.

138
00:07:16.633 --> 00:07:18.633
Here we go.

139
00:07:18.633 --> 00:07:20.800
You can always drag
your visualizations around

140
00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.133
if you don't like the way
that they initially get rendered.

141
00:07:23.133 --> 00:07:25.500
You can see our start
event goes to step one

142
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.233
and it can either go to a first event,

143
00:07:27.233 --> 00:07:29.533
which triggers step two
and the rest of the flow,

144
00:07:29.533 --> 00:07:32.633
or it can emit a loop event
which looks back to step one.

145
00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.033
Your loop could be anywhere,
so we could have decided to loop back

146
00:07:36.033 --> 00:07:39.333
from step three or step two,
and it would have worked equally well.

147
00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.500
You don't just have to loop back
to the same step that you started from.

148
00:07:42.733 --> 00:07:46.633
The same constructs that allow you to loop
allow you to create branches.

149
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.800
Here's a workflow that executes

150
00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:52.400
two different branches
depending on an early decision to make it.

151
00:07:52.633 --> 00:07:55.600
First, we're going to need
some additional events.

152
00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.300
We're going to call them branch A1,

153
00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.600
branch A2, branch B1, and branch B2.

154
00:08:00.933 --> 00:08:04.800
We're going to create a new branch
workflow that uses those events

155
00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:08.233
and has more steps
than our previous workflow did.

156
00:08:10.033 --> 00:08:13.033
It has the
same start with a random number generator,

157
00:08:13.333 --> 00:08:18.100
but now it emits either
a branch A1 event or a branch B1 event.

158
00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.600
A1 will trigger step A1,
which will trigger step A2.

159
00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:26.800
B1 will trigger step B1,
which will trigger step B2.

160
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.000
You don't actually need to instantiate
the workflow to visualize it.

161
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.000
You can just pass the workflow class
directly to the visualizer.

162
00:08:33.033 --> 00:08:36.033
So let's do that this time.

163
00:08:36.533 --> 00:08:39.433
And see what a branching workflow
looks like.

164
00:08:39.433 --> 00:08:42.433
We'll display our HTML.

165
00:08:45.333 --> 00:08:46.900
And we can zoom in and out.

166
00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:49.900
If our visualization is too big
for the window.

167
00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:54.300
As you can see, we've got our start event
going to step A1 to step A2.

168
00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:58.233
Or if we omit the branch B1 event,
we go to step B1 and step B2.

169
00:08:58.633 --> 00:09:01.633
Both of them
go to a stop event and are complete.

170
00:09:01.633 --> 00:09:04.133
So now we've got a couple forms
of flow control.

171
00:09:04.133 --> 00:09:07.033
The final form of flow control
that you can implement in workflows

172
00:09:07.033 --> 00:09:09.033
is concurrent execution.

173
00:09:09.033 --> 00:09:12.633
This allows you to efficiently run
long-running tasks in parallel

174
00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.133
and gather them together
when they are needed.

175
00:09:15.133 --> 00:09:18.133
This can also let you perform
MapReduce-style tasks.

176
00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:19.833
Let's see how this is done.

177
00:09:19.833 --> 00:09:22.800
You'll be using a new concept
the context object.

178
00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.200
This is a form of shared memory available
to every step in a workflow.

179
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:27.433
To access it

180
00:09:27.433 --> 00:09:31.000
declared as an argument to your step
and it will be automatically populated.

181
00:09:31.233 --> 00:09:36.233
In this example, you'll use context dot
send event rather than returning an event.

182
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.300
This allows you to omit
multiple events in parallel

183
00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.300
rather than just returning one as you did
previously.

184
00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.633
So we've created a step two events.

185
00:09:44.633 --> 00:09:48.333
We've created a parallel flow class,
and we've changed our start

186
00:09:48.333 --> 00:09:51.333
event
to accept the context parameter here.

187
00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:54.000
This is going to automatically populate
our context.

188
00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.133
And we're calling send event three times.

189
00:09:56.133 --> 00:09:58.900
It's emitting three different step
two events.

190
00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:02.400
One was query one,
one was query two and one was query three.

191
00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.833
We've set numb workers for here.

192
00:10:04.833 --> 00:10:06.100
This is also the default.

193
00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.400
It's just to show you
how to set this parameter.

194
00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.633
This can set how many of these step twos
will run in parallel.

195
00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:16.900
What step two does is it simply waits a
random number of seconds between 1 and 5,

196
00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:18.800
and then it returns a stop event

197
00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.033
which will emit the query name
that we set in step two event.

198
00:10:24.133 --> 00:10:26.700
So let's execute that.

199
00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.700
So now let's execute the workflow.

200
00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:32.433
As you can see it ran all three queries.

201
00:10:32.433 --> 00:10:34.400
And then it output query one.

202
00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.800
And then it halted.

203
00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:38.800
You don't see it
outputting query two and query three.

204
00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.033
That's because the very first stop event
that fires halts the workflow.

205
00:10:43.033 --> 00:10:47.300
If we run this again, we'll probably get
a different query ending the workflow.

206
00:10:47.933 --> 00:10:48.633
There you go.

207
00:10:48.633 --> 00:10:50.833
This time, query three won the race.

208
00:10:50.833 --> 00:10:53.833
So that's
how you execute things in parallel.

209
00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:54.900
But what if you

210
00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.433
wanted the output of all three queries
and not just the first one to complete?

211
00:10:58.633 --> 00:10:59.533
Another method

212
00:10:59.533 --> 00:11:03.033
context dot
collect events exists for that purpose.

213
00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.800
So let's see what this workflow
looks like.

214
00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:10.100
We've created a new step three event
and a new concurrent workflow.

215
00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.433
Just like before,
we're omitting three step two events,

216
00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.633
each of which emits a query one,
query two, and query three.

217
00:11:16.633 --> 00:11:21.600
And just like before, we have step two
that waits and random number of seconds.

218
00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:25.833
We've modified it to emit a step
three event which passes the query.

219
00:11:25.933 --> 00:11:30.933
Step three is going to be triggered
every single time a step three event runs.

220
00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.633
But we're using the Collect events method

221
00:11:34.633 --> 00:11:37.600
to wait for certain number of events.

222
00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.800
How collect events works
is every time it runs,

223
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.200
if it hasn't seen the right number
of events, then it will return none.

224
00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:47.700
So that's why we're handling a none here.

225
00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:50.700
It means that not
all of the events have been received yet.

226
00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:51.600
When it receives

227
00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.633
the full number of events that it needs,
it will return them in an array.

228
00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.900
So that's what we're doing here,
is we're printing the result.

229
00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:01.633
Let's execute this workflow
to see what it looks like.

230
00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:08.900
As you can

231
00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:12.933
see, it prints not all events received yet
twice before

232
00:12:12.933 --> 00:12:14.733
It has received all three events.

233
00:12:14.733 --> 00:12:19.733
And then it emits the events, it emits
them in the order that it received them.

234
00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.333
So every time you run it,
these will be in a different order,

235
00:12:23.333 --> 00:12:27.700
because the steps happen a random
number of seconds after each other.

236
00:12:28.033 --> 00:12:30.733
Let's execute it one more time
just to see that happen.

237
00:12:30.733 --> 00:12:34.200
Not all events received yet, and now
the results are in a different order.

238
00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:35.333
Cool.

239
00:12:35.333 --> 00:12:40.333
So this works if you want to receive
a number of events of the same type.

240
00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.800
However, you can use branching
to emit different

241
00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.800
types of events and wait for all of them
to happen separately.

242
00:12:46.933 --> 00:12:51.533
So let's give an example of how
you would collect different event types.

243
00:12:51.933 --> 00:12:55.533
For this, we're going to create
a whole bunch of different event types.

244
00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:59.533
Step A event, step a complete, step
B event, step b complete, and so on.

245
00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:03.100
And now we're going
to create a new workflow.

246
00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:05.900
Just like

247
00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:09.700
before this one uses
send event to emit events.

248
00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:13.133
Except we've changed the type definition
here to say

249
00:13:13.133 --> 00:13:17.400
that it can emit a StepAEvent,
or a StepBEvent, or a StepCEvent.

250
00:13:17.533 --> 00:13:20.200
And here we're emitting
all three of those types of events.

251
00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:22.633
We've got a step
that accepts each of those.

252
00:13:22.633 --> 00:13:26.433
So there's a step A, a step B, and a step C
which collect those.

253
00:13:26.633 --> 00:13:30.633
All of them emit a step A complete, step
B complete, step C event,

254
00:13:30.633 --> 00:13:32.333
Step C complete event.

255
00:13:32.333 --> 00:13:33.900
And then in step three

256
00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:37.633
we've told it that it should accept
all three of those types of events.

257
00:13:37.933 --> 00:13:40.800
Now our collect events function

258
00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:43.800
has been modified to say collect events

259
00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:47.100
but collect a step C, a step
A, and a step B.

260
00:13:47.433 --> 00:13:51.133
The order of these is significant
as you'll see in a second. Here,

261
00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:53.233
we do as we did before.

262
00:13:53.233 --> 00:13:56.300
If the events that we've collected
are none, that means that we haven't

263
00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:59.300
collected all of our events yet,
so we simply return none.

264
00:13:59.733 --> 00:14:03.033
This is what happens if all three of
the results have been collected.

265
00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:04.433
So let's execute this cell

266
00:14:05.533 --> 00:14:07.100
to define the workflow.

267
00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:10.000
And then run the workflow.

268
00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.800
As you can see, it did something A ish,
something B ish, something C ish.

269
00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.633
It received all three queries.

270
00:14:15.633 --> 00:14:18.633
And then when it prints out the results
you'll see that

271
00:14:18.733 --> 00:14:23.133
the array contains step C complete,
step A complete, and step B complete.

272
00:14:23.133 --> 00:14:24.933
The order is the same as the order

273
00:14:24.933 --> 00:14:27.433
that we defined
when we were defining collect events.

274
00:14:27.433 --> 00:14:30.833
That is how you know which event you
will get when you're getting them back.

275
00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:33.300
This new workflow
has quite a pretty visualization,

276
00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.300
so I'm going to visualize it.

277
00:14:39.133 --> 00:14:42.133
As usual, we display the HTML.

278
00:14:43.933 --> 00:14:46.933
It's useful to be able to drag
these things around and reorder them,

279
00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:50.300
so that you can make things
a little bit easier to understand.

280
00:14:50.433 --> 00:14:53.433
In this case,
if you drag them like this, you get

281
00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.500
this really quite pleasing visualization.

282
00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:58.500
In practical use,

283
00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.300
agents can take a long time to run.

284
00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:04.300
It's a poor user experience
to have the user execute a workflow,

285
00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:07.100
and then wait a long time
to see if it works or not.

286
00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:08.700
It's better to give them some indication

287
00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:12.000
that things are happening in real time,
even if the process is not complete.

288
00:15:12.133 --> 00:15:16.500
To do this, workflows
allows streaming events back to the user.

289
00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:21.400
Here, you'll use context dot writes
event to stream to emit these events.

290
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.300
This is going to be the first time
that we actually use an LLM

291
00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:29.600
to do something in this tutorial so far,
so let's make sure that we bring in

292
00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.100
OpenAI's LLM.

293
00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:34.100
We're also going to define our events
for a new workflow.

294
00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:35.233
There's a first event,

295
00:15:35.233 --> 00:15:38.333
a second event,
a text event, and a progress event.

296
00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.600
You'll see what those are useful
for later.

297
00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:41.633
The specific event

298
00:15:41.633 --> 00:15:44.933
that we're going to be sending back
is the Delta response from the LLM.

299
00:15:45.033 --> 00:15:45.700
When you ask

300
00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:49.500
an LLM to generate a streaming response,
as you're going to do in a second,

301
00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:53.200
it sends back each chunk of its response
as it becomes available.

302
00:15:53.233 --> 00:15:54.900
This is available as the delta.

303
00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:59.100
What we're going to do is wrap the delta
in a text event and send it

304
00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.733
back to the workflows own stream,
so that you can echo it out to the user.

305
00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:04.700
Let's see what this workflow looks like.

306
00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:08.700
In step one, we're writing our first event
to a stream a progress event

307
00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:10.600
that just says that step one is happening.

308
00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:14.433
In step two, we instantiate
the LLM using GPT-4o-mini.

309
00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:16.900
We're calling LLM dot a stream

310
00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:21.533
complete. The a is significant,
which means it's an asynchronous function

311
00:16:21.533 --> 00:16:25.433
as opposed to just the stream
complete event, which would be blocking.

312
00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:27.500
Calling it gives us a generator.

313
00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:30.500
We can use the generator to emit

314
00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:33.400
events from the LLM as they happen.

315
00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:37.200
Every time we get a response from the LLM,
we can call write

316
00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:40.500
event to stream and emit
a text event to our own stream.

317
00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:44.000
We're passing a delta
that is the delta from the response.

318
00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:47.700
Once the generator is complete,
we're going to return a second event,

319
00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:51.700
which means that
the entire generation is complete.

320
00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:55.233
And then we're going to call step
three and emit one more progress event.

321
00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:59.900
So, let's execute this function.

322
00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:03.000
It's going to be slightly different
this time

323
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.700
because instead of just running
the workflow and getting a response,

324
00:17:07.033 --> 00:17:10.233
we're going to run the workflow
and handle the stream of events.

325
00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:13.233
So instead of awaiting workflow dot run,

326
00:17:13.233 --> 00:17:17.400
we're going to call workflow dot
run directly and get a handle from it.

327
00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.800
We're then going to call Stream
events on the handler.

328
00:17:21.100 --> 00:17:24.100
This gives us every event
that the workflow emits.

329
00:17:24.533 --> 00:17:27.533
Because there can be a lot of events
emitted from a workflow,

330
00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:30.600
you'll want to filter it down
to just the ones you're interested in

331
00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:32.500
by using the type of the event.

332
00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:35.500
So in this case, we're looking for
two types of events:

333
00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:38.733
those progress events that we emitted, in
which case we just print

334
00:17:38.733 --> 00:17:41.733
the message that comes with them
or the text events that we emitted,

335
00:17:41.833 --> 00:17:44.900
in which case we print the delta
that we received.

336
00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:47.000
The end equals parameter here

337
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.200
make sure
that these all end up on one line,

338
00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:50.400
because otherwise you end up

339
00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:53.000
with each chunk on a different line,
which is hard to read.

340
00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:56.000
We get the final result
by using await on the handler,

341
00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:59.300
and then we can print the final result,
which was emitted from step three.

342
00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:01.333
Let's see that happening in practice.

343
00:18:04.533 --> 00:18:05.900
You see step one happens.

344
00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:10.900
And now you can see that the results
of the call to the LLM appeared in chunks.

345
00:18:11.533 --> 00:18:14.533
They didn't appear all at once.

346
00:18:14.733 --> 00:18:16.800
I can run that again just to be

347
00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:19.533
just to demonstrate.

348
00:18:19.533 --> 00:18:20.933
There you go.

349
00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:22.533
So step one is happening.

350
00:18:22.533 --> 00:18:24.433
Then you get all of the chunks
from the response.

351
00:18:24.433 --> 00:18:27.233
Then step three is happening.
And then the final result.

352
00:18:27.233 --> 00:18:28.400
Congratulations.

353
00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:29.600
You've successfully built

354
00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:33.600
a number of increasingly complex workflows
and you've mastered the basic concepts.

355
00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:36.500
In the next lesson,
you'll add RAG to your workflow.

